# Core Philosophy

Prioritize scalable, maintainable, production-oriented React architecture over short or clever solutions.

Assume the project may grow significantly in complexity over time.

Avoid beginner-only patterns that do not scale well in real applications.

Never prioritize beginner simplicity over scalable architecture.

---

# Teaching Style

The user is learning React but already has programming experience.

Explain:
- why architectural decisions matter
- responsibilities between layers
- important tradeoffs briefly

Avoid oversimplified patterns.

Avoid unnecessary enterprise overengineering.

---

# Separation of Concerns

Separate:
- UI rendering
- client state
- server state
- API communication
- async state
- business logic
- derived state

Avoid mixing multiple responsibilities inside the same component.

---

# Component Design

Prefer:
- small focused components
- single responsibility
- composition over prop drilling
- reusable hooks
- reusable utility functions

Avoid:
- god components
- deeply nested conditionals
- duplicated UI logic

Prefer early returns.

---

# Derived State

Do not store derived state unnecessarily.

Compute derived values from source state.

Prefer pure utility functions for:
- filtering
- sorting
- transformations
- formatting

---

# Naming

Use intention-revealing names.

Prefer:
- fetchMoodEntries
- useCommentsQuery
- createPostMutation

Avoid vague names:
- data
- stuff
- temp
- handleThings

---

# File Structure

Prefer scalable frontend structure:

src/
  components/
  pages/
  hooks/
  services/
  stores/
  lib/
  query/
  router/
  utils/

Avoid unrelated responsibilities in the same file.

---

# Performance

Do not prematurely optimize.

Prioritize:
- readability
- maintainability
- predictable behavior

before micro-optimizations.

Use memoization only when justified.

---

# Code Quality

Prefer explicit code over clever shortcuts.

Avoid unnecessary abstractions.

Refactor repeated patterns when appropriate.

Write code that remains easy to modify months later.